From: Salem874 <Salem874@MWBMpartners.ltd>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 00:40:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?fix(ci):=20security-propagate=20fails=20closed?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20=E2=80=94=20only=20a=20real=20empty=20cherry-pick=20is=20ski?=
 =?UTF-8?q?pped;=20push/PR=20failures=20open=20the=20backport=20issue=20(#?=
 =?UTF-8?q?109=20follow-up,=20#121)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two faults in the step that copies a merged security/dependency fix from
main onto alpha, beta and release-candidate, both found by an independent
review and both reproduced locally before fixing:

1. Genuine failures were mistaken for "already applied". The #109 check
   treated any failed cherry-pick that left the files untouched as an
   "empty" pick (the fix is already on that branch) and skipped it with no
   issue. But some git errors stop before changing anything — a stale lock
   file, or an untracked file in the way (git exit code 128) — and those
   were silently swallowed too. The check now also requires that a
   cherry-pick is really in progress (CHERRY_PICK_HEAD exists), which is
   only true when git stopped part-way. Every other failure opens the
   "Manual backport needed" issue, which says whether it was a conflict or
   another git error and gives git's exit code.

2. A refused push, or a failed pull request, left no trace except a red
   run. GitHub's built-in token is never allowed to push changes to
   workflow files, and every candidate so far has been a Dependabot update
   to workflow files, so the push would fail every time. The push, the
   "is a PR already open?" check, and PR creation are now each guarded:
   if one fails, the backport issue is opened explaining which step failed
   (pointing at #121 for the long-term token fix), and the run still ends
   red.

Also:
- Every "fix did not reach the branch" outcome now ends the run red after
  the issue is filed — including a plain conflict, which used to end green.
  Deliberate: a green run hides the problem from anyone scanning the
  Actions list (the #93-#95 lesson).
- Re-running a failed run comments on the already-open issue instead of
  opening a duplicate (quoted-title search, then an exact-title match; if
  the search itself fails, a new issue is opened rather than none).
- The issue text uses only the PR number, branch names, the commit id,
  git's exit code, a run-log link and fixed wording — never the PR title
  or git's messages. Untrusted values still reach the script only through
  env:.
- Header comment rewritten in plain English to describe all of the above.
  Triggers, permissions, the job gate and the pinned actions are unchanged.

Tested locally (GitHub Actions cannot run at the moment): the step's run:
script, extracted from the YAML, was run for the old and new versions
against throwaway git repositories with a fake gh — 16 scenarios, all
passing for the new script, on macOS (git 2.55, bash 5.3) and in an
Ubuntu 24.04 container (git 2.43, bash 5.2). actionlint and shellcheck are
clean. Not yet reviewed by a second system (Codex).

Refs #109, #121

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .github/workflows/security-propagate.yml | 154 +++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 127 insertions(+), 27 deletions(-)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/security-propagate.yml b/.github/workflows/security-propagate.yml
index f3fb6fd..7fea5a0 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/security-propagate.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/security-propagate.yml
@@ -8,11 +8,37 @@
 # that would leave the other three branches exposed.
 #
 # This workflow closes that gap: when a security/dependency PR is MERGED into
-# `main`, it cherry-picks the merged commit onto `alpha`, `beta`, and
+# `main`, it cherry-picks (copies) the merged commit onto `alpha`, `beta`, and
 # `release-candidate` and opens a PR against each — so the same fix reaches
-# every branch. A cherry-pick conflict opens a tracking ISSUE for manual
-# backport instead of failing silently. An EMPTY cherry-pick (the fix is
-# already on that branch under a different commit) is skipped quietly (#109).
+# every branch.
+#
+# 🚦 When the copy can't be made, it FAILS CLOSED — if in doubt, it asks a
+#    human rather than quietly carrying on:
+#    • Fix already on that branch under a different commit (an "empty"
+#      cherry-pick, e.g. Dependabot's own per-branch PR already landed it) →
+#      skipped quietly, run stays green (#109). This is ONLY believed when git
+#      really did stop part-way through a cherry-pick (CHERRY_PICK_HEAD exists)
+#      with nothing conflicted and nothing changed. Until 2026-09-25 the
+#      CHERRY_PICK_HEAD check was missing, so ANY git error that happened to
+#      leave the files untouched (a stale lock file, an untracked file in the
+#      way — git exit code 128) was mistaken for "already applied" and
+#      silently swallowed.
+#    • A conflict, or any other cherry-pick error → a "⚠️ Manual backport
+#      needed" ISSUE is opened (with git's exit code) and the run ends RED.
+#    • The copy worked but pushing the branch, or opening the PR, failed →
+#      the same issue, saying which step failed, and the run ends RED. The
+#      usual cause today: GitHub's built-in token (GITHUB_TOKEN) is never
+#      allowed to push changes to `.github/workflows/*`, and most Dependabot
+#      fixes ARE workflow updates. Until 2026-09-25 the job just died red
+#      with no issue at all. The long-term fix (a dedicated GitHub App or
+#      token that may push workflow files) is tracked in #121.
+#    • Re-running a failed run does not pile up duplicate issues: if the
+#      issue for that PR + branch is already open, a comment is added to it
+#      instead.
+#    The issue text only ever contains the PR NUMBER, branch names, the
+#    commit id, git's exit code, a link to the run log and our own wording —
+#    never the PR title or git's messages (those can carry text we don't
+#    control; the linked run log has them).
 #
 # 🔒 Safety: uses `pull_request_target` (needs write scope to push branches +
 #    open PRs) but only ever cherry-picks an ALREADY-MERGED commit from `main`
@@ -88,6 +114,52 @@ jobs:
           git config user.name 'github-actions[bot]'
           git config user.email '41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com'
 
+          # 📋 One tracking issue per (PR, target branch). The title is built
+          #    only from values we trust (the PR *number* and our own matrix
+          #    target), never the PR title, so it is safe to reuse below.
+          ISSUE_TITLE="⚠️ Manual backport needed: propagate PR #${PR_NUMBER} to ${TARGET}"
+          # Link to this run's log — GitHub sets these variables on every run
+          # (the ":-" defaults only stop `set -u` killing the script if not).
+          RUN_URL="${GITHUB_SERVER_URL:-https://github.com}/${GITHUB_REPOSITORY:-}/actions/runs/${GITHUB_RUN_ID:-}"
+
+          # 🚨 The fix did NOT reach ${TARGET}: record that where a human will
+          #    see it, then end the run RED.
+          #    $1 = plain-English account of what went wrong. Callers pass ONLY
+          #         our own wording plus trusted values (branch names, exit
+          #         codes) — never the PR title or git's error text.
+          #    Re-runs: if the issue is already open, comment on it instead of
+          #    opening a duplicate. The search is quoted-phrase, and the result
+          #    is then filtered to an EXACT title match, because GitHub search
+          #    is word-based and could return a near-miss. If the search itself
+          #    fails we open a new issue — a duplicate beats no issue at all.
+          report_backport_needed() {
+            local what="$1"
+            local body
+            local existing
+            body="$(printf '%s\n\n%s\n\n%s\n\n%s' \
+              "Automatic propagation of security/dependency fix #${PR_NUMBER} (merged into \`main\`) onto \`${TARGET}\` did **not** finish. Source commit: \`${MERGE_SHA}\`." \
+              "**What went wrong:** ${what}" \
+              "Please backport the fix to \`${TARGET}\` by hand, so that branch is not left without it." \
+              "Run log (shows git's own messages): ${RUN_URL}")"
+            if ! existing="$(gh issue list --state open \
+                --search "\"${ISSUE_TITLE}\" in:title" \
+                --json number,title \
+                --jq "[.[] | select(.title == \"${ISSUE_TITLE}\")][0].number // empty")"; then
+              echo "::warning::Could not search for an existing tracking issue — opening a new one."
+              existing=""
+            fi
+            if [ -n "${existing}" ]; then
+              gh issue comment "${existing}" --body "Still needed — this run failed again. ${body}"
+            else
+              gh issue create \
+                --title "${ISSUE_TITLE}" \
+                --body "${body}" \
+                --label automated --label security --label dependencies
+            fi
+            echo "::error::${MERGE_SHA} was NOT propagated to ${TARGET} — tracking issue opened/updated."
+            exit 1
+          }
+
           # Target branch may not exist yet (e.g. release-candidate not created).
           if ! git ls-remote --exit-code --heads origin "${TARGET}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
             echo "::notice::Target branch '${TARGET}' does not exist — skipping."
@@ -112,34 +184,62 @@ jobs:
           MFLAG=()
           if [ "${PARENTS}" -ge 3 ]; then MFLAG=(-m 1); fi
 
-          if git cherry-pick "${MFLAG[@]}" "${MERGE_SHA}"; then
-            git push -u origin "${BRANCH}" --force-with-lease
-            if gh pr list --head "${BRANCH}" --state open --json number --jq 'length' | grep -qx 0; then
-              gh pr create \
-                --base "${TARGET}" \
-                --head "${BRANCH}" \
-                --title "[auto-propagate → ${TARGET}] ${PR_TITLE}" \
-                --body "Automated propagation of security/dependency fix #${PR_NUMBER} (merged into \`main\`) onto \`${TARGET}\`, so the fix lands on every branch in the alpha → beta → release-candidate → main model. Source commit: \`${MERGE_SHA}\`. Review + merge once CI is green." \
-                --label automated --label security --label dependencies
-            else
-              echo "::notice::PR for ${BRANCH} already open — updated the branch."
-            fi
-          else
-            # 🧹 An "empty" cherry-pick is NOT a conflict. If the target branch
+          # Capture git's exit code rather than using `if git cherry-pick`, so
+          # the failure branch below can report it.
+          PICK_EXIT=0
+          git cherry-pick "${MFLAG[@]}" "${MERGE_SHA}" || PICK_EXIT=$?
+
+          if [ "${PICK_EXIT}" -ne 0 ]; then
+            # 🧹 An "empty" cherry-pick is NOT a failure. If the target branch
             #    already carries the same change (e.g. Dependabot's per-branch
             #    version PR landed it separately, as a different commit), git
-            #    stops with "nothing to commit". With no conflicted paths and a
-            #    clean index + working tree, there is genuinely nothing to
-            #    back-port — skip quietly instead of opening a false-alarm
-            #    "Manual backport needed" issue (#109; see #93/#94/#95).
-            if [ -z "$(git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U)" ] && git diff --cached --quiet && git diff --quiet; then
+            #    stops part-way with "nothing to commit" (#109; see #93-#95).
+            #    ALL FOUR must hold before we believe that:
+            #      1. a cherry-pick really is in progress (CHERRY_PICK_HEAD) —
+            #         without this, errors that stop git BEFORE it changes
+            #         anything (exit 128: stale lock file, untracked file in
+            #         the way, …) also look "clean" and were being skipped;
+            #      2. no conflicted paths;  3. clean index;  4. clean files.
+            #    Anything else fails closed: tracking issue + red run.
+            #    (`|| true`: if listing conflicts itself fails, the checks
+            #    below still refuse to call it empty.)
+            CONFLICTED="$(git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U || true)"
+            if [ -z "${CONFLICTED}" ] \
+              && git rev-parse -q --verify CHERRY_PICK_HEAD >/dev/null \
+              && git diff --cached --quiet \
+              && git diff --quiet; then
               git cherry-pick --skip || git cherry-pick --abort || true
               echo "::notice::${MERGE_SHA} is already applied on ${TARGET} (empty cherry-pick) — skipping."
               exit 0
             fi
             git cherry-pick --abort || true
-            gh issue create \
-              --title "⚠️ Manual backport needed: propagate PR #${PR_NUMBER} to ${TARGET}" \
-              --body "Automatic propagation of security/dependency fix #${PR_NUMBER} to \`${TARGET}\` hit a **cherry-pick conflict** (source commit \`${MERGE_SHA}\`). Please backport it to \`${TARGET}\` manually so the fix is not left out of that branch." \
-              --label automated --label security --label dependencies
+            # File names and git's message are deliberately left out of the
+            # issue (see header); the run log has them.
+            if [ -n "${CONFLICTED}" ]; then
+              report_backport_needed "Copying the fix onto \`${TARGET}\` hit a **cherry-pick conflict** (git exit code ${PICK_EXIT}): the change clashes with something already on that branch."
+            fi
+            report_backport_needed "Copying the fix onto \`${TARGET}\` failed with a git error that was **not** a conflict (git exit code ${PICK_EXIT}). The run log shows git's message."
+          fi
+
+          # ✅ The fix is now committed on ${BRANCH}. Publish it and open the
+          #    PR. Each step is guarded so that a failure opens the tracking
+          #    issue instead of just killing the job (`set -e`) with nothing
+          #    left to show for it.
+          if ! git push -u origin "${BRANCH}" --force-with-lease; then
+            report_backport_needed "The fix was copied onto \`${TARGET}\` cleanly, but **pushing the branch \`${BRANCH}\` failed**. If this change edits files under \`.github/workflows/\`, that is almost certainly why: GitHub's built-in token (GITHUB_TOKEN) is never allowed to push changes to workflow files. The long-term fix — a dedicated GitHub App or token for this workflow — is tracked in #121."
+          fi
+          if ! OPEN_PRS="$(gh pr list --head "${BRANCH}" --state open --json number --jq 'length')"; then
+            report_backport_needed "The branch \`${BRANCH}\` was pushed, but **checking for an existing pull request failed**, so the workflow could not tell whether one is already open and did not open one. If there is none, open one from that branch into \`${TARGET}\` by hand."
+          fi
+          if [ "${OPEN_PRS}" = "0" ]; then
+            if ! gh pr create \
+              --base "${TARGET}" \
+              --head "${BRANCH}" \
+              --title "[auto-propagate → ${TARGET}] ${PR_TITLE}" \
+              --body "Automated propagation of security/dependency fix #${PR_NUMBER} (merged into \`main\`) onto \`${TARGET}\`, so the fix lands on every branch in the alpha → beta → release-candidate → main model. Source commit: \`${MERGE_SHA}\`. Review + merge once CI is green." \
+              --label automated --label security --label dependencies; then
+              report_backport_needed "The branch \`${BRANCH}\` was pushed, but **opening the pull request failed**. Open one from that branch into \`${TARGET}\` by hand."
+            fi
+          else
+            echo "::notice::PR for ${BRANCH} already open — updated the branch."
           fi
-- 
